  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>I. Number of electrons in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>+</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></math></p><p>II. Number of electrons in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>NH</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></math></p><p>III. Number of electrons in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></math></p><p>IV. Number of electrons in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></math></p><p>Since, species with same number of electrons are called isoelectronic species, Hence, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>IInd</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>IIIrd</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>IVth</mi></math> are isoelectronic.</p>",
